course: geography
lesson: 64
title: Olympiad: which country am I? ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A "which country am I?" chain gives clues one by one, and each clue crosses some countries off the list until only one is left, just like a detective ruling out suspects.
explain: The trick is to start from the sharpest clue: "my capital was chosen in 1923 and my biggest city sits on two continents" points straight at Turkey, while "I am in Asia" alone leaves dozens.
explain: Many chains hide a trap about capitals, because the most famous city is often not the capital: Sydney is not Australia's capital, Dubai is not the capital of the United Arab Emirates, and Casablanca is not Morocco's.
explain: Other chains join two lessons: a clock clue ("my time is UTC plus 5 hours 45 minutes") and a mountain clue ("the highest peak on Earth is on my border") together can only mean Nepal.
explain: Picture a quiz night in a Black Country pub, where "bostin'" means brilliant: the quizmaster says "I'm massive, I've got 11 time zones and a bit of me is stuck between Poland and Lithuania", and the bostin' answer is Russia.
fact: Turkey's capital is Ankara, chosen in 1923, while its largest city, Istanbul, lies on both sides of the Bosporus, in Europe and in Asia.
fact: Australia's capital is Canberra, built as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne, and the UAE's capital is Abu Dhabi, not Dubai.
fact: Nepal uses UTC plus 5 hours 45 minutes, its capital is Kathmandu, and Mount Everest stands on its border with China.
fact: Russia is the largest country in the world, uses 11 time zones, and its Kaliningrad region lies cut off between Poland and Lithuania.
fact: Uzbekistan and Liechtenstein are the only two doubly landlocked countries, and Samarkand and Bukhara, great Silk Road cities, are in Uzbekistan.
fact: Chile is about 4,300 km long but on average less than 200 km wide, between the Andes and the Pacific, and holds the Atacama, the driest non-polar desert.
fact: South Africa has three capitals, Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein, and the whole country of Lesotho lies inside it.
fact: Bolivia is named after Simón Bolívar; its official capital is Sucre, while its government sits in La Paz, the highest seat of government in the world.
fact: Thailand, with its capital Bangkok, is the only country in South-East Asia never colonised by a European power.
fact: The Philippines, capital Manila, are named after King Philip II of Spain, and Spain ruled them from 1565 to 1898.
fact: Kinshasa, capital of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Brazzaville, capital of the Republic of the Congo, face each other across the Congo River.
fact: Mongolia is a huge landlocked country with about 3.5 million people, and its capital Ulaanbaatar is the coldest national capital on Earth on average.
fact: Ukraine is the largest country wholly in Europe, because only about a quarter of Russia's land is in Europe.
exercise: I am landlocked, I am in Asia, and my capital is the coldest national capital on Earth. Who am I? | Mongolia | Kazakhstan | Nepal | Russia
exercise: My capital was chosen in 1923, and my largest city stands on two continents. Who am I? | Turkey | Egypt | Russia | Greece
exercise: My clocks run at UTC plus 5 hours 45 minutes, and the highest mountain on Earth is on my border. Who am I? | Nepal | India | China | Bhutan
exercise: I am long and thin, the Andes are my eastern wall, and I hold the driest non-polar desert. Who am I? | Chile | Ecuador | Argentina | Bolivia
exercise: I have three capitals, and a whole country lies inside me. Who am I? | South Africa | Italy | The United Kingdom | Switzerland
exercise: I am in South-East Asia, my capital is Bangkok, and no European power ever ruled me. Who am I? | Thailand | Vietnam | The Philippines | Malaysia
exercise: I am named after a Spanish king, and Spain ruled me for over three hundred years. Who am I? | The Philippines | Bolivia | The Dominican Republic | Colombia
exercise: I am named after a liberator, and my government sits higher than any other in the world. Who am I? | Bolivia | Ecuador | Venezuela | Peru
exercise: I am doubly landlocked and Samarkand is mine. Who am I? | Uzbekistan | Liechtenstein | Kazakhstan | Mongolia
exercise: My capital looks across a river at another country's capital, and that other capital is Kinshasa. Who am I? | The Republic of the Congo | The Democratic Republic of the Congo | Egypt, across the Nile | Angola
exercise: Which country is the odd one out: Kazakhstan, Mongolia, Bolivia, Chile? | Chile, the only one with a coast | Mongolia, the only one in Asia | Bolivia, the only one with mountains | Kazakhstan, the only big one
exercise: Which country's capital is also its largest city? | The United Kingdom | Australia | The United Arab Emirates | Morocco
exercise: Which is the largest country lying wholly in Europe? | Ukraine | Russia | France | Germany
exercise: I am the largest country in the world, and part of me lies between Poland and Lithuania. Who am I? | Russia | Germany | Belarus | Lithuania
exercise: Which pair are both doubly landlocked? | Uzbekistan and Liechtenstein | Mongolia and Switzerland | Nepal and Bolivia | Kazakhstan and Austria
exercise: Which of these is a capital city? | Abu Dhabi | Dubai | Sydney | Casablanca
exercise: Which clue alone is enough to name Nepal? | Its clocks run at UTC plus 5 hours 45 minutes | It is a country in Asia | It is a country with high mountains | It borders China
test: Quizmaster in Dudley: "I'm massive, I've got 11 time zones and a bit stuck between Poland and Lithuania. Bostin' clue, that." Who? | Russia | China | Canada | The United States
test: Mate: "Australia's capital is Sydney, everyone knows that, bruv." Right? | No, it is Canberra | Yes, it is Sydney | No, it is Melbourne | No, it is Perth
test: "I'm a country, I'm on two continents, my big city's split by a strait less than 1 km wide, and my capital's NOT that city." Who? | Turkey | Kazakhstan | Morocco | Denmark
test: Cousin from Glasgow: "Wee clue for ye: I'm fair skinny, Andes on one side, ocean on the other, my north is never dreich (grey and drizzly) cos it barely ever rains." Who? | Chile | Peru | Bolivia | Argentina
test: Lad reckons Lesotho is just a bit of South Africa, not its own country. Right? | No, it is a separate country inside South Africa | Yes, it is one of South Africa's three capitals | Yes, it is a South African province | No, it lies inside Botswana
test: "No cap, Dubai is the capital of the UAE, I've literally been there a million times." What's the fix? | Abu Dhabi | It's Dubai, he's right | The capital is Doha | The capital is Muscat
test: Friend: "Bolivia's capital is La Paz, end of." What's the fuller answer? | Sucre is the official capital, but the government sits in La Paz | La Paz is the only capital and always was | The capital is Lima, high in the Andes | Bolivia has no capital at all
test: Clue chain: "I'm in Asia. I'm landlocked. I'm NOT a stan. My capital's dead cold." Who? | Mongolia | Kazakhstan | Nepal | Uzbekistan
test: "Yeah nah, Thailand was ruled by Britain like the rest round there," says a mate in Brisbane. Right? | No, Thailand was never colonised by Europeans | Yes, Thailand was British until 1965 | Yes, Thailand was ruled by France | No, Thailand was ruled by Spain
test: Sis: "Uzbekistan's only a bit landlocked, like it's not that deep." What does "doubly landlocked" really mean? | Every neighbour is landlocked too | It is landlocked twice as much as others | It has two seas inside it | It borders two oceans
test: Quiz card: "My capital and my neighbour's face each other across Africa's deepest river." Which river? | The Congo | The River Nile | The River Niger | The Zambezi
exam: Identify the state whose seat of government is situated at the greatest altitude of any in the world, notwithstanding that its official capital is located elsewhere. | Bolivia | Ecuador | Nepal | Peru
exam: Determine which of the following sovereign states constitutes the sole country of South-East Asia never subjected to European colonial rule. | Thailand | Vietnam | Indonesia | Malaysia
exam: A state possesses three capital cities and wholly encloses another sovereign state. Ascertain its identity. | South Africa | Italy | Switzerland | Nigeria
exam: Select the country whose name commemorates a sixteenth-century Spanish monarch. | The Philippines | Colombia | Bolivia | Argentina
